12. [x] Discovery content index — implemented
    Every scan re-reads and re-hashes each book, and `books::candidate` also runs
    `prepare::prepare` per book only to learn the optimized variant's identity,
    then throws the result away. Nothing is cached; there is no mtime check.
    Measured here: 10 books of 3 MB take 4.4 s cold and 4.4 s again on r; the
    same library with --no-optimize takes 1.4 s, so about two thirds of the time
    is discarded image re-encoding. One 18 MB book costs 1.55 s to optimize.
    A 500-book library is roughly 3.7 minutes of CPU on every launch and every
    refresh, single-threaded per location.
    Cache identity per (place, path, size, mtime) in the XDG cache directory,
    recording the optimize/organized options that produced it so a settings
    change invalidates the entry. A hit must only supply identity for a file the
    current scan actually enumerated: this stays a fresh inventory, never an
    offline history. The cache must be best-effort — unreadable, corrupt or
    unwritable never fails a scan. Also compute the optimized variant lazily,
    when a device copy that might match it actually appears.
    Implemented in `src/cache.rs`: a versioned, best-effort JSON index keyed by
    (place, path, size, mtime, optimize, organized) for local files, plus an
    optimized-variant identity keyed by content hash, which also spares Kobo
    books the image re-encoding without needing the lazy scheme above. Entries
    expire after 90 days and are capped at 20000, most recently used kept.
    Writes are atomic; a missing, corrupt, foreign-version or unwritable index
    degrades to recomputation. Measured on the same 10 books of 3 MB: cold
    4.6 s unchanged, repeat scan 0.01-0.04 s, one modified book 0.10 s.
    Tests cover reopening, corruption, an unwritable location, key
    invalidation, and that a cached book whose file is gone never appears.

13. [x] Device scans reuse known identities — implemented
