    discovery decrypts every book into a temporary directory on every scan.
    `Reader::files` returns only path, size and directory, so identity cannot be
    derived remotely, but we already know the identity of everything we uploaded
    and (path, size) is a sound key for a device we do not otherwise write to.
    With item 12 in place, a refresh against an unchanged reader should cost one
    listing call.
    Implemented: `kobo::Library::source_path` exposes the device file each book
    is imported from, and discovery fingerprints it exactly as a local file, so
    an unchanged Kobo book is never decrypted again. Reader and card books are
    keyed by the path and size the listing reports, scoped to that device or
    mount, so they are not downloaded again. A same-size replacement is not
    detected, which is recorded in the README and in a test. Measured on ten
    3 MB books on a card: 0.73 s cold, 0.01 s warm.

14. [x] Stream discovery results and parallelize within a location — implemented
    `books::scan` emits a snapshot only when a whole location finishes, so a
    large local scan shows nothing for minutes and then everything at once.
    Emit every N books or every 250 ms; the model already merges partial
    snapshots. `progress(snapshot.clone())` deep-clones the whole catalog per
    emission, which gets expensive as emissions get more frequent. Within a
    location books are processed one at a time; the work is CPU-bound and
    independent, so a pool across books should scale with the core count.
    Implemented: locations report each book as they find it, `scan` merges
    incrementally and publishes a location's own result immediately, the first
    books at once and the rest at most every 200 ms. `merge` no longer sorts on
    every insertion, which was quadratic once books arrived one at a time;
    snapshots are ordered when they are emitted. Local books are read by a pool
    of up to eight threads. On four cores, ten 3 MB books: cold scan 4.6 s to
    2.2 s, and the first book is visible after 0.7 s rather than at the end.
    Kobo and reader books are still read one at a time: `rusqlite::Connection`
    is not Sync, so a pool would need one library per thread and a copy of the
    device database each, and parallel downloads risk overwhelming the reader.

