19. [x] macOS verification and published releases — done
    macOS verification is done: see the section below. Packaging on a `v*` tag
    already exists and produces Linux and both macOS archives as Actions
    artifacts, and the v0.1.0 tag ran it: ubuntu-24.04, macos-15 and
    macos-15-intel all passed and produced archives with checksums.
    A packaged macOS binary still has not executed anywhere. On the user's
    corporate MacBook the quarantine attribute cannot be cleared, so an unsigned
    download will not run there at all; building from source on that Mac works
    and reports 0.1.0. Confirming a Mac archive runs therefore needs either an
    unmanaged Mac or signing and notarization.
    Remaining: decide whether to publish GitHub Releases at all, knowing that
    the macOS archives in them are unsigned and unrunnable on managed devices.
    Settled: publish them. The premise was wrong. macOS does not quarantine any
    download — it quarantines what a quarantine-aware application writes, which
    is a browser, Mail or AirDrop. curl, wget and git do not set the attribute,
    and Gatekeeper only refuses files that carry it. That is why Homebrew's
    formula bottles run unsigned and unnotarized: they are plain tarballs its
    own downloader fetched. Casks are the other case, signed by the vendor
    rather than by Homebrew. On Apple Silicon a binary needs a signature to
    execute at all, but an ad-hoc one satisfies that, costs nothing and is
    applied by the toolchain at link time; it is not notarization.
    So the release job now takes every platform's artifacts rather than only
    Linux's — they were being built and tested on every tag and then left out —
    and both the release notes and the README carry a curl install for macOS,
    saying plainly that a browser download is the thing that breaks.
    Still true, and still said in both places: no packaged macOS binary has been
    run on a Mac. Publishing it does not make it verified. A managed Mac may
    also enforce a policy stricter than Gatekeeper's default, which curl would
    not get around; that is one machine's problem rather than a reason to
    publish nothing.
    Verified on 2026-09-20. The aarch64 archive CI built for the v0.1.1 tag was
    moved to a Mac with scp — which sets no quarantine attribute, as curl does
    not — and ran, reporting its version. So an unsigned, unnotarized package
    executes on a Mac with no exception made for it, and the toolchain's ad-hoc
    signature is all Apple Silicon wanted. The earlier failure on a corporate
    MacBook was a browser download's quarantine, not the binary.
    It was the managed MacBook that ran it, which is the strongest form this
    answer comes in: that machine's restrictions stop quarantine being cleared
    and nothing else. Homebrew works on the same machine, and its formula
    bottles are unsigned and unnotarized for exactly the reason established
    here, so it had been running unsigned binaries there all along.
    The manual said a packaged macOS binary had been produced by CI and never
    executed. That is no longer true and no longer said, in the manual, the
    README or the release notes.
    Closed by v0.2.0. The release job took every platform's artifacts and
    published nine assets: an archive, a checksum and a source archive for
    Linux, Apple Silicon and Intel macOS. The one-line installer then fetched
    and verified it on both Linux and the managed Mac, which is the last link
    that had never been walked — the mkdir that BSD install needs and the PATH
    note that macOS needs had only been reasoned about until then.
    The first tag failed and was worth it. The Intel macOS runner hit
    EWOULDBLOCK in the ADEPT test's own fulfillment server: a socket accepted
    from a non-blocking listener inherits that flag on BSD and macOS but not on
    Linux, so a read returned immediately whenever the request had not already
    arrived, and a read timeout cannot help a socket that never waits.
    tests/crosspoint.rs already carried that fix, with a comment; the ADEPT
    server was the one place it had not been applied. Re-running the job would
    have hidden it. The tag was re-pointed rather than bumped, since the failed
    run published nothing and there was nothing for anyone to have fetched.

