22. [x] Metadata beyond title and author — implemented
    `Book` is title, author and copies, which is why sorting offers title and
    author and nothing else. `epub::metadata` already parses the OPF metadata
    block and discards everything but two fields. Carry series and index,
    language, publisher and date; sort by series; accept `author:` and
    `series:` prefixes in the search box. Series is the one that matters: a
    library of any size sorted by title interleaves every series it has.
    A PDF says who wrote it and what it is called, in its information
    dictionary, and all of it was being thrown away: every PDF was titled by its
    filename, had no author, and so converted into a book filed under "Unknown
    author" on the reader. That is now read, decoded from UTF-16 where a byte
    order mark says so, and carried into the EPUB as dc:creator, which is where
    the author folder comes from. Both files here now read as "Agentic AI /
    Singhal, Abhi" and "Programming as Theory Building / Peter Naur, Alistair
    Cockburn", and land in folders of those names. A conversion is also named
    after the book rather than after the PDF's filename.
    Series is read from both generations — Calibre's `calibre:series` and
    `calibre:series_index`, and EPUB 3's `belongs-to-collection` refined by
    `group-position` — and carried on the book through the cache, which moves to
    version 6. `s` now cycles title, author and series rather than toggling two,
    and a series sorts in its own order: the position is padded so 2 comes
    before 10 and 1.5 sits between them, and a book in no series sorts after
    every book in one. The details panel names the series and the number.
    Search takes a field before a colon — `author:`, `title:`, `series:` —
    which searches that field alone, so `author:herron` does not also match a
    book written about him.
    Since verified end to end, without waiting for a library that uses series:
    seven EPUBs were built carrying it both ways — Calibre's `calibre:series`
    pair and EPUB 3's `belongs-to-collection` refined by `group-position` — and
    run through the real binary rather than through tests. Both forms parse, the
    sort puts Slow Horses 1, Dead Lions 2, The List 2.5 and Spook Street 10 in
    that order rather than sorting 10 next to 1, a book in no series lands last,
    the details panel names the series and number, all three field searches
    match only their own field, and the series survives the identity cache and
    comes back from it.
    Found while doing it: Ctrl+U in the search box typed the letter u rather
    than clearing, because any Char was pushed to the query without looking at
    its modifiers. A control chord is a command, not text. The settings panel
    already cleared on Ctrl+U; the search box now does too, and the help says
