23. [x] Free space before a copy — implemented
    Marking forty books and pressing Enter discovers a full card partway
    through. For a mounted card or a Kobo this is one statvfs and a line in the
    copy dialog: the size of the set against the room left. `Status` is version
    and device only, so Wi-Fi has no free-space endpoint to ask; that path must
    say unknown rather than guess, as location states already do.
    Implemented with fs2, already a dependency, so nothing new was added.
    `books::room` takes the path a place writes to and walks up to the nearest
    existing parent, so a folder yet to be made reports the disk it will sit on.
    The dialog reads all three places once when it opens rather than on every
    frame it is drawn for, and shows the room beside each destination. A set
    weighing more than the room left is marked in red rather than refused: the
    weight is taken from the sources, which is the most it could need, since
    optimizing and converting only ever make a book smaller.
    Confirmed rather than assumed: CrossPoint has no endpoint for storage.
    /api/status carries a free heap, which is memory, and info, storage, disk,
    system, free, space, fs and device all answer Not Found. So a reader over
    Wi-Fi says free unknown, and only a mounted card, a Kobo or a local folder
    is measured. Verified against a real card at 6.5 GiB free; the unknown path
    has only a render test behind it, because the reader left the network while
    it was being checked.

24. [x] A record of what was copied — implemented
    Every copy is verified and nothing is ever overwritten, but nothing
    remembers any of it. An append-only journal — what went where, when,
    verified or failed — and `crossload history` to read it, with the last few
    entries in the TUI. Completes the trust the rest of the tool has already
    paid for, and is the precondition for ever undoing a sync.
    Implemented in `src/history.rs` as JSON lines in the state directory, which
    is where something that is not disposable belongs — the cache is next door
    and is. `books::transfer` is the one road every copy takes, so the record is
    kept there, around a renamed inner function, and it covers the TUI, `sync`
    and `kobo sync` at once. Writing is a courtesy: a copy that happened is
    never reported as failed because its line could not be written, an
    unparseable line is skipped rather than spoiling the record around it, and
    the file is trimmed to its last 5000 entries only once it has grown past
    them. `crossload history` takes -n, --failed and --json; h shows the same
    in the library, newest first, with failures in red.
    The only date arithmetic in the program is here, written out rather than
    pulled in: days from the civil epoch by Hinnant's algorithm, tested against
    a leap day and against 2000, which is a leap year where 1900 was not.
    Verified by copying real books and reading the record back, including a CBZ
    refused by the reader, which the record keeps with its reason.

25. [~] Kobo annotations and collections — reading done, --organized not
    `kobo.rs` opens KoboReader.sqlite and reads only the `content` table. The
    same database holds `Bookmark` (highlights and notes) and `Shelf` /
    `ShelfContent` (collections). Export highlights per book as Markdown:
    read-only, risks nothing, reuses a connection that is already open. Books
    are not the only thing trapped on a closed device, which is the same reason
    the ADEPT work exists. Collections follow: shelves are a better basis for
    `--organized` than author names.
    `Library::annotations` reads Bookmark joined to content, so a mark arrives
    with the book it was made in. Hidden marks are left where they are, and so
    are dog-ears: a dog-ear marks a page and says nothing, so there is nothing
    in it to carry off. A database without the table at all is a device with no
    marks rather than an error. `crossload kobo notes` prints Markdown grouped
    by book, `--output DIR` writes one file each and never overwrites one,
    `--json` gives it as data. The book's words are quoted and the reader's are
    not. Read-only throughout: a Kobo's database is the device's, not ours.
    Verified against the real Kobo here — 13 highlights across four books, with
    its 14 dog-ears correctly left behind.
    Shelves are read too, by `Library::shelves`. `crossload kobo shelves` lists
    the collections and the books on each, as a table or `--json`, and
    `crossload kobo list --shelf NAME` narrows a listing to one, matching the
    name however it is typed and naming the collections that do exist when the
    one asked for does not. ShelfContent keys on Shelf.InternalName, which is
    the Name for a collection made on the device; a schema with no InternalName
    column is read by Name instead. Deleted shelves and deleted shelf entries
    are text "true" rather than booleans, and both are dropped. An empty shelf
    is still a shelf, and an entry whose book has left the device is named by
    what is left of it. Shelf.Type is deliberately not filtered on: a real
    device was the only place to learn whether it needed to be, and it did not.
    Verified against the real Kobo here — seven collections over 75 books,
    exactly the seven it shows, including the empty one. Reading it found one
    bug: an entry with no content row has a NULL title, which SQLite sorts
    before everything, so the ordering now uses the name actually shown.
    `--organized` still groups by author. Using collections for it changes the
    folder layout on a device, which is a decision about someone's reader
    rather than a detail of this, and the reading side above is what it would
    be built on. Open questions when it is wanted: a book on several shelves
    has to go somewhere or be copied twice, and a book on none needs a home.

26. [x] kepub for the Kobo — implemented
