27. [~] PDF to EPUB — done but for pictures
    Many PDFs have no reason to be PDFs. Many others cannot survive the trip,
    and a bad conversion is a book you do not find out is unreadable until you
    are away from your computer. So the feature is the triage, not the
    converter: classify before offering. Convertible is born-digital, one text
    column, an embedded outline, consistent margins. Poor is multi-column,
    footnote-heavy or full of tables — offer with a warning. Refuse page images
    with no text layer; OCR is a different project. Every signal is cheap from
    the page objects: text layer present, x-position clustering of text blocks,
    strings repeating at a fixed y (running headers to strip), an outline to
    become chapters.
    MIT licensing rules out MuPDF (AGPL) and Poppler (GPL). Pure Rust (lopdf,
    pdf) or pdfium (BSD-3, but a Chromium-scale C++ build). The output half
    already exists: zip, roxmltree and image are dependencies and `prepare`
    already writes EPUB containers. What is missing is PDF parsing plus reflow —
    paragraph joining from line gaps, dehyphenation, headings from font size.
    Convert alongside the PDF, never over it; keep both, show both, and let the
    result be read before it is sent anywhere. Depends on item 21.
    Classifier spiked in `src/pdf.rs` on lopdf 0.45 (MIT, pure Rust, no C++
    toolchain; its tree adds nom, indexmap, weezl and the like). It reads page
    structure only — where text sits, never what it says — and reports pages,
    pages carrying text, image-only pages, pages whose text starts in two
    separated bands, and whether an outline exists. A page is two-column when a
    left margin and a second margin past the middle both carry text and the
    gutter between them is quiet.
    Two things had to be right before the numbers meant anything, and neither
    was obvious. MediaBox is usually an indirect reference, so a page's width
    has to be dereferenced or every band is measured against the wrong scale.
    More importantly, Td and TD move in text space: their offsets are in units
    of the text matrix's own scale, so a jump of 20 at a scale of 11 is 220
    points. Added raw, the jump to the next column collapses to 20 and every
    page looks like one column. Both are covered by tests.
    Measured on real files: a 40-page two-column collection reported 35 of 38
    text pages as two-column once the scaling was right, against 13 before, and
    an 8-page single-column report came back convertible with an outline. Both
    verdicts match what pdftotext -layout shows.
    Conversion implemented on top of it: `pdf::convert` returns EPUB bytes and
    the verdict that went with them. `text.rs` is one walk of the content stream
    serving both judging and converting, so the two can never disagree about a
    page; `layout.rs` rebuilds prose from placed text; `write.rs` writes an
    EPUB 3 with an anchor per heading and a stable identifier, so converting the
    same PDF twice gives the same book. Text is decoded through the font's own
    encoding and ToUnicode CMap, bounded against decompression bombs.
    Three more things had to be right, each found by reading real output rather
    than by reasoning. Advance widths must come from the font: simple fonts
    carry `Widths` from `FirstChar`, composite fonts carry `W` on their
    descendant, and a composite font codes two bytes per glyph, so a byte count
    puts every run at twice its width. A space drawn as its own run must be kept
    rather than discarded as whitespace, because the words either side are
    otherwise left to be separated by the gap the space was holding open. And
    the short-line rule only means something in justified text: in ragged text
    every line ends somewhere different, so it is applied only when most lines
    end near the margin.
    Columns are read one at a time and the second is slid onto the first's
    margin, so indent and line-ending tests see one column. Reading a
    two-column page straight across weaves the columns together, which is the
    single worst thing a conversion can do; that is now a test. Because columns
    are handled, being in columns is no longer a concern on its own — only
    changing between columned and not, page to page, where the split is least
    certain.
    Measured on real files: a 40-page two-column collection converts to prose
    that reads correctly, and an 8-page report converts cleanly with its
    outline. Both pass Crossload's own EPUB validation and appear as ordinary
    books in the catalog, titled from the PDF's own metadata.
    Hyphens and chapters since settled. A hyphen left by a line break is
    decided by what the document does elsewhere: it is its own dictionary, and
    a compound it hyphenates where it had room will appear that way, while a
    word it broke appears whole. real-time survives and inconsis-tent closes up,
